{"step_index":214,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-18T07:25:44Z","input_tokens":2307,"cache_read_tokens":373494,"output_tokens":7802,"thinking":"The output confirms the Cryptomatte data is present, specifically the `CryptoObject` layer. This indicates successful generation of Cryptomatte information during the render process, confirming the initial hypothesis regarding render layer output.\n\n\nThe Cryptomatte output validates the render layer's successful Cryptomatte data generation. The approach ensures a pixel-perfect, anti-aliased hero-car matte at no added computational expense. This includes associated 3D speed FX objects for razor-sharp compositing.\n\nThe strategy encompasses designing all three V6 scripts for Spider-Verse quality, accounting for every item in both specified references.  The initial focus is on `pipeline/v6_sv_comp.py`, which manages the transition from lookdev to animation while maintaining a safe \"On Twos\" (12 fps) animation rhythm, without any chase cam functionality.\n\n\nThe primary concern is eliminating judder by managing different animation frame rates. Maintaining world-space position data for the car and cameras at 24 fps ensures smooth motion. Car-local secondary motion, like suspension and steering, should be stepped at 12 fps to achieve a stylized, hand-drawn look.\n\n\nThe brake heat should be stepped on twos. Considering wheel spin, at 70 m/s, the wheel rotates approximately 1.3 times per frame. Using constant interpolation on wheel spin and 2-frame decimation, the spokes and comic smear arcs will appear as crisp poses matching the 12 fps graphics of Spider-Verse. The constant interpolation will keep the wheel smear arcs from blurring with motion blur.\n\n\nThe constant interpolation approach is confirmed as the correct method to implement stepped animation on twos (12 fps). This will be applied to material telemetry actions like brake heat and shift LEDs, ensuring they update consistently on every other frame. Further analysis will concentrate on animating the 3D speed FX collection (`V6_09_ComicFX`) at 12 fps, examining each object within this collection.\n\n\nFocus is now on achieving the desired comic flutter effect for the 3D ribbons by stepping the scale and roll animation at 12 fps, creating a hand-drawn style. Camera safety is paramount; front-wing and cockpit FX must never obstruct the cockpit camera view, particularly on specified shots, adjusting visibility based on sightline intersections.\n\nThe wheel-spin comic smear arcs will be animated, their visibility and scale governed by speed, and incorporating stepped 12 fps angular jitter to make the arcs flicker around the tires. The render's motion blur shutter will be reduced, with a corresponding adjustment to the camera's f-stop for achieving crisp frames with subtle streaks.\n\n\n`DOF` cameras will be clamped to a maximum value, <= 16.0, prioritizing the Spider-Verse CMYK Print Misregistration effect. The frame timelines in references #25 and #26 will be cross-checked, ensuring visual and audio cuts align precisely.\n\nThe `scene[\"sv_dimension\"]` keyframes will use constant interpolation with 2-frame glitch transitions: Act I (`f = 1..788`) uses `sv_dimension = 0.0`. A portal cut will occur from f=788 to 791, with a glitch in `sv_glitch` and `sv_dimension` change (0.0 -> 2.0 -> 1.0). Act II (`f = 789..1601`) sets `sv_dimension = 1.0`.\n\n\nAnalyzing scene transitions and applying specific visual effects. The goal is to establish the visual style described in the references using keyframe animation and parameter adjustments. Specifically, portal cuts and graphic impact frames are being introduced, with precise timing aligned to reference audio cues.\n\n\nFocusing on scene effects, the intent is to produce specific visual flashes timed precisely with audio cues: white-hot/ink-black flashes with chromatic shockwaves, followed by graphic crimson/obsidian stencils. These effects will be timed precisely with audio cues, specifically sub-bass drops in clip 3. The aim is to create high contrast silhouettes, razor sharp, as per the given references.\n\nAnalysis moves to verifying the Blender compositor setup, examining the node and pass connections within `Comp_V6_SpiderVerse`. This will ensure passes are enabled for correct image processing.\n\n\nInitial setup includes adjusting mist settings to enhance depth and visual impact alongside enabling various render passes, specifically Z, emission, and Cryptomatte object data. The Cryptomatte node isolates the hero car's Cryptomatte using specified object names. The resulting background is then clamped, preparing it for additional processing.\n\nAnisotropic Kuwahara filter adds a painterly effect to the background, parameterized by sharpness and eccentricity. This filter's size is animated across two acts, transitioning from a subtle texture in Act I to a pronounced watercolor effect in Act II. The blending is controlled using mist data and a smoothstep function to define how the background impact is visually controlled.\n\n\nKuwahara filter parameters are being adjusted across the acts to manage the background's visual effect, enhancing the anti-aliasing on the hero car matte. Further, the filter's sharpness and blending weight will be parameterized for each act, transitioning to a sharper effect. The edge detection method will use the Kirsch and Sobel filters on normal and mist passes respectively to extract graphic ink lines.\n\nThese ink lines are combined using a threshold ramp to create crisp silhouettes and creases, with ink weight scaled dynamically based on camera distance to prevent aliasing, particularly on distant objects. The hero car ink is adjusted based on an animated scale to prevent clumping in telephoto shots, guaranteeing consistent detail at all focal lengths.\n\n\nFocusing on achieving specific visual effects across different animation shots. Implementing ink lines for comic panels by applying slight ink color shifts per universe in Acts I, II, and III. Calculating an off-focus factor for the CMYK effect. This factor modulates the red/cyan print misregistration, allowing the hero car to always have zero misregistration. During portal glitch cuts and impact frames, vertical scan and tear offset spikes will be added.\n\n\nApplying an off-focus factor to simulate CMYK print misregistration, shifting Red channel by `(+X_shift, +Y_shift)` and Blue by `(-X_shift, -Y_shift)`, leaving Green channel anchored. This maintains hero car sharpness while introducing color fringes to out-of-focus elements, achieving the desired comic print effect.\n\nUsing bloom to enhance the comic glow on brake rotors, rain light, shift LEDs, and aero ribbons. A comic paper grain texture is being integrated, scaled to render size, and softly blended to add texture to the composite.\n\nThe grain effect's blend weight will be adjusted to simulate the paper tooth across the acts. Considering also adding high-speed radial border speed-lines and 2-frame graphic impact duotone to further enrich the visual style.\n\n\nIntegrating a comic paper grain texture, adjusting the blend weight to simulate paper tooth across acts, including flat gradients.  Creating radial border speed-lines, sized to the screen's outer 28%, driven by animated speed. Smoothly activates when car speed exceeds 288 km/h, subtly pulsing in a 12 fps frame-alternating phase.\n\nThe radial speed lines are animated by flipping the X/Y scale on a 4-frame cycle (constant every 2 frames), animating a single packed image around screen edges at high speed, achieving a hand-drawn look. A duotone impact frame stage is also being added.\n\n\nFocusing on creating comic impact frames. Implementing a three-tone stencil based on scene luminance, emission, the car matte, and radial speed lines. This involves mapping these elements to pure paper white, Spider-2099 crimson, and pitch ink black. This transition creates impactful, hand-inked comic frames at specific time points. Also integrating a CLI renderer to process frames.  The renderer has options for frame ranges, output directories, and samples.  The renderer will update the scene's camera on every frame to avoid camera errors, and can also run Blender's animation renderer.\n\n\nBlender's animation render directly writes frames sequentially using the expected naming pattern, confirmed by matching output file names. This is verified by testing multiple animation ranges. Camera switching is handled automatically at timeline markers. Explicitly setting the active camera prior to rendering ensures correct camera assignment even for partial shot renders, which verifies the prior hypothesis. This solves the camera issue.\n\n\nInitial concern centers on Blender camera behavior during animation rendering with timeline markers. Questioning whether markers are scanned retroactively to the `frame_start` or if the initial camera setting prevails until the next marker. Solution is to explicitly set the active camera before rendering, ensuring correct camera assignment at the start of any animation, even partial shot renders.\n\nFocus shifts to generating 26 stylized 1080p stills for the project. These stills will cover specific visual styles associated with different acts and dimensions, adhering to precise file naming conventions specified in a reference document. Consideration is given to a possible additional \"impact inset\" still for a poster, with a plan to either extract it from an existing frame or render a new frame.\n\n\nFocusing on crafting a multi-panel comic book page poster with a specific layout and visual style using Pillow and NumPy. This involves generating aged paper borders, CMYK halftone gradients, and a custom comic masthead. The layout comprises seven angled and asymmetric panels separated by gutters and hand-inked borders.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"read_board","args":{"since":27}},{"name":"write_file","args":{"Content":"# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\n\"\"\"\nV6 SPIDER-VERSE RENDERER — 支持分段并行渲染与全片 2161 帧渲染\n输入: v6_spiderverse/v6_stage_anim_sv.blend\n\n用法:\n  blender -b -P pipeline/render_v6_sv.py -- --range 61-156 --outdir renders_v6/clips_frames/clip1_miles_1610\n  blender -b -P pipeline/render_v6_sv.py -- --range 1290-1385 --outdir renders_v6/clips_frames/clip2_gwen_65\n  blender -b -P pipeline/render_v6_sv.py -- --range 1650-1745 --outdir renders_v6/clips_frames/clip3_2099_impact\n  blender -b -P pipeline/render_v6_sv.py -- --all\n\"\"\"\nimport bpy\nimport os\nimport sys\nimport time\n\nPROJ = r\"D:\\项目session\\blender-supercar\"\nARGV = sys.argv[sys.argv.index(\"--\") + 1:] if \"--\" in sys.argv else []\n\n\ndef argval(flag, default=None):\n    if flag in ARGV:\n        i = ARGV.index(flag)\n        if i + 1 < len(ARGV) and not ARGV[i + 1].startswith(\"--\"):\n            return ARGV[i + 1]\n        return True\n    return default\n\n\nBLEND = argval(\"--blend\", os.path.join(PROJ, \"v6_spiderverse\", \"v6_stage_anim_sv.blend\"))\nRANGE_STR = argval(\"--range\", None)\nDO_ALL = bool(argval(\"--all\", False))\nSAMPLES = int(argval(\"--samples\", 16))\nOVERWRITE = bool(argval(\"--overwrite\", True))\n\nif not os.path.isabs(BLEND):\n    BLEND = os.path.join(PROJ, BLEND)\n\nbpy.ops.wm.open_mainfile(filepath=BLEND)\nsc = bpy.context.scene\n\nif DO_ALL and not RANGE_STR:\n    f_start, f_end = 1, sc.frame_end\n    outdir = argval(\"--outdir\", os.path.join(PROJ, \"renders_v6\", \"frames\"))\nelif RANGE_STR:\n    parts = str(RANGE_STR).split(\"-\")\n    f_start, f_end = int(parts[0]), int(parts[1])\n    outdir = argval(\"--outdir\", os.path.join(PROJ, \"renders_v6\", \"frames\"))\nelse:\n    raise SystemExit(\"Specify --range START-END [--outdir DIR] or --all\")\n\nif not os.path.isabs(outdir):\n    outdir = os.path.join(PROJ, outdir)\nos.makedirs(outdir, exist_ok=True)\n\n# 确保从任意中间帧起渲时，初始活动相机与最近的前置 Timeline Marker 严格一致\nmarkers = sorted(sc.timeline_markers, key=lambda m: m.frame)\ninit_cam = sc.camera\nfor mk in markers:\n    if mk.frame <= f_start and mk.camera:\n        init_cam = mk.camera\n    elif mk.frame > f_start:\n        break\nif init_cam:\n    sc.camera = init_cam\n\nsc.render.engine = 'BLENDER_EEVEE'\nsc.eevee.use_raytracing = False\nsc.eevee.taa_render_samples = SAMPLES\nsc.render.resolution_x = 1920\nsc.render.resolution_y = 1080\nsc.render.resolution_percentage = 100\nsc.render.fps = 24\nsc.frame_start = f_start\nsc.frame_end = f_end\nsc.render.use_motion_blur = True\nsc.render.motion_blur_shutter = 0.07\nsc.render.image_settings.file_format = 'JPEG'\nsc.render.image_settings.quality = 95\nsc.render.filepath = os.path.join(outdir, \"f_\")\nsc.render.use_overwrite = OVERWRITE\nsc.render.use_placeholder = not OVERWRITE\n\nt0 = time.time()\nprint(f\"V6 RENDER START: frames {f_start}..{f_end} ({f_end - f_start + 1}f) -> {outdir} (init_cam={sc.camera.name if sc.camera else None})\", flush=True)\nbpy.ops.render.render(animation=True)\nelapsed = time.time() - t0\nn_frames = max(1, f_end - f_start + 1)\nprint(f\"V6 RENDER DONE: {n_frames} frames in {elapsed:.1f}s ({elapsed / n_frames:.2f} s/frame)\", flush=True)\n","Overwrite":true,"Path":"d:\\项目session\\blender-supercar\\pipeline\\render_v6_sv.py"}}]}
